sasl-xoauth2 (0.29) unstable; urgency=medium

  Security:
  * Keep token file permissions when updating a token file (updated files
    used to be created according to the umask, usually world-readable);
    write token files atomically and durably, never leaving a partial file;
    when written by root, token and lock files keep their owner.
  * Token requests: URL-encode all parameters, only use HTTPS, do not follow
    redirects, require TLS 1.2, time out (10s connect, 30s total), limit
    the response size.
  * Do not send an empty client_secret. Send scope only for client
    credentials requests (refresh requests keep the granted scopes).
  * Do not send the client_secret from the configuration file to a token
    endpoint, proxy or CA set in a token file; such a file needs its own.
  * Reject user names and tokens containing control characters; escape
    control characters and cut long values (e.g. provider responses) in
    logs; limit token lifetimes; parse numbers strictly.
  * Hardened build by default (RelWithDebInfo, _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, stack
    protector, full RELRO); the plugin exports only sasl_client_plug_init.
  * The configuration file is installed with mode 0640 and never
    overwritten by a later installation.

  Reliability:
  * Fix refresh_window in the configuration file being ignored (and "0"
    being written into every token file, which is now ignored); default
    300 seconds, maximum 1800.
  * Serialize token refreshes between processes with a lock file
    (<token file>.lock) and reuse a token another process just obtained.
  * Use a new token even if it cannot be stored (and log an error); keep
    using a still-valid token if a refresh shortly before expiry fails.
  * Failures log one line naming the token file, endpoint, HTTP status,
    provider error (e.g. AADSTS code), a hint, and the plugin version.
  * Never terminate the mail system's process from within the plugin (no
    exit(), no exceptions leaving the plugin); a configuration that fails
    validation is not used.
  * An unreadable expiry in a token file only causes a refresh; setgid
    directories keep the token file's group.

  Microsoft 365:
  * New grant_type and scope options: app-only access with the client
    credentials flow (e.g. SMTP.SendAsApp), as an alternative to the
    delegated refresh token flow, which remains the default.
  * Client credentials can authenticate with a certificate (recommended;
    client assertion signed with PS256 as specified by Microsoft, options
    client_certificate_file and client_private_key_file) or a client secret;
    client_secret is optional in the configuration file. Needs OpenSSL.

  sasl-xoauth2-tool and test-config:
  * New command refresh-token (test-config --update): requests and stores a
    new token under the plugin's lock; run daily (systemd timer or cron) to
    keep unused refresh tokens alive and to check the setup.
  * get-token outlook: --client-credentials with --certificate/--private-key
    (checks the setup right away) or a client secret, --mailbox; switching
    a token file between these and the delegated flow removes settings that
    no longer apply. No longer requests the unneeded openid scope.
  * test-token-refresh/refresh-token exit with 1 (transient failure) or 2
    (needs action), also check that the token directory is writable, and
    ignore proxy environment variables, as Postfix does.
  * --version (-v, -V) shows the versions and paths of the tool, its helper
    and the installed plugin, and warns about a mix of versions; the help
    shows the version and lists the commands; test-config has -h/--help.
  * Token files: created readable by their owner only, owner and group kept
    when overwriting, locked while updating, symlinks refused; '-' writes to
    stdout; real token expiry; for Gmail, only the fields the plugin uses.
  * Authorization code flows use state and PKCE; the Gmail listener binds to
    127.0.0.1 (--local-listen-address) and serves only Google's redirect;
    --login-base must be https; Entra ID error details are shown; works on
    Python 3.6 again.
  * CA certificate script: removes certificates no longer present on the
    system, copies symlink targets, skips dangling symlinks.

  Build:
  * Requires OpenSSL (libssl-dev) and CMake 3.13; -DEnableTests=OFF no
    longer builds the test program.

  Documentation:
  * README: build, update and upgrade instructions for Ubuntu 26.04 LTS
    (including removing older installations); both Microsoft 365 ways
    (delegated, app-only with certificate or secret) side by side; shared
    mailboxes; verified TLS (smtp_tls_security_level = secure,
    smtp_tls_CAfile, smtp_tls_loglevel); permissions; on Ubuntu 26.04,
    ReadWritePaths=-/etc/tokens instead of the previously recommended
    ProtectSystem=true; running in production (checklist, Postfix
    settings, daily token refresh, monitoring, backups); searching the
    logs; troubleshooting table; logging options.
  * Man page sasl-xoauth2.conf(5): new options, token file keys, value
    syntax.

  Repository:
  * Clone URL and man page links point to LMS235/sasl-xoauth2; references
    to pre-built packages and FUNDING.yml removed; disclaimer extended.

 -- Florian Lamml <info@florian-lamml.de>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0200

